    passageEn: 'In the pitch-black bathypelagic abyss, where sunlight never penetrates, bioluminescence represents the prevailing form of illumination. Over seventy percent of deep-sea marine animals generate biological light through endogenous chemical reactions or symbiotic photobacteria. Anglerfish dangle glowing cephalic lures to lure prey into cavernous toothy jaws. Other abyssal organisms, like lanternfish and comb jellies, produce brilliant cyan flashes to disorient voracious predators. Bioluminescent communication enables deep ocean fauna to mate, hunt, and camouflage against the dim downwelling glow.',
