    passageEn: 'Glaciers are colossal, slow-moving rivers of recrystallized ice that exert tremendous transformative power on mountain terrain. Over successive centuries, massive snow accumulations compress into dense blue ice that creeps down valleys under its own immense weight. As a glacier crawls forward, basal meltwater freezes into rock crevices, plucking massive boulders from valley floors. Embedded rocks scour the underlying bedrock like coarse sandpaper, grinding narrow V-shaped gorges into majestic, steep-walled U-shaped valleys. Glacial retreats leave behind sparkling alpine lakes, jagged arêtes, and sculpted granite peaks that define mountain majesty.',
